A||N||8||wxxo||if your tranny went out what would you do?||||1||
Z||000000||wxxo||07-14-2000||06:18 PM||||86 se v6 4spd <BR>im not sure what im going to do. heres the ling and short of it...was driving to work today and as i was looking at the speedo it went down to zero. i pulled over to make sure that the sending unit hadnt come off. it hadnt. 2 miles up the road at a stop light the engine dies but starts right up. no history of that ever happening. so i take it to the shop and they tell me that i need a new tranny. they said that the gear had fallen off of the sending unit went down into the gearbox and messed a bunch of stuff up. im thinking how much damage can a little plastic sprocket to to metal gears but who knows. question is what type of tranny should i look into getting?? money is an issue. should i stay with the 4 spe or try an isuzu 5 speed?? tia||||reg||1
Z||000001||TheFiero64||07-14-2000||06:52 PM||||I would go with the Isuzu 5 speed. I have that trans in my 88 coupe V6. It shifts smooth and has good gearing for that engine. Think of the gas you would save when your in 5th gear idling at 50 MPH :-). On the other hand I don't see how the gear fell inside. I have had a 4 speed that was on an 86 SE and the gear fell off, but there is a ridge that it will land on. The only time it can fall inside the tranny itself is when you pull the sending unit out and then it can go sideways and into the tranny. The plastic gear probably would get chopped to bits inside.  If they said it fell inside it was them that dropped it in there. Good luck<P>------------------<BR>Joshua Bumpus<BR>New Bedford, MA<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fi.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Taijiguy||07-15-2000||12:11 AM||||If you're doing things yourself, open the case and see what's going on. Sounds to me like that shop is just trying to screw you, and I don't mean where you get a nice wet kiss to go along with it. There's not really any way that gear can fall "off" with the sending unit in place, unless the metal shaft from the unit broke, and that caused the damage. I think if it were me, and the plastic gear broke, but left the shaft, before removing anything, (like the trans) I'd drain the fluid and look it over for plastic chips. If I found some, I'd probably fill the transmission with kerosene, lift a wheel, put it in gear, and let it run for a couple of minutes to wash the inside of the tranny, then drain and fill with oil/atf *whichever you prefer) And replace the gear.<P>Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000003||SCCA FIERO||07-15-2000||04:28 AM||||I have to agree with Fiero64 on the Isuzu. I have it in my 88 Coupe strapped to a V6 and I love it. It supposedly can't handle the V6, but believe me, it will!<P>My old 86SE (it donated it's engine to my current 'moneypit') had the M17 4 speed and it pulled hard 1st through 2nd then had nothing for 3rd or 4th. The gearing is too far apart. The Isuzu pulls very hard in 1st, 2nd, 3rd (3rd feels almost like 2nd) and 5th will take you to 70mph at about 2500 rpm's!<P>------------------<BR>-Chris <BR>88 Fiero Coupe<BR>V6 Fastback||||reg||1
Z||000004||BBBAD GT||07-15-2000||08:15 AM||||Which shop did you take it to? How long did it take them to diagnose the problem?  Just curious......<P>------------------<BR>87 GT, Stock for now<BR><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT's Web Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||07-15-2000||09:43 AM||||Think I agree with Taijiguy's advice. Can't comment on the kerosene rinse, because I haven't tried it.  But,just because a speedometer quits and the engine stalls once, this shop says you need a new transmission??<P>If money is an issue (and you said it is), get the car away from the repair shop (it still drives, right?).  Replace the speedometer gear, change fluid and be on your way.<p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 07-15-2000).]||||reg||13
Z||000006||wxxo||07-15-2000||01:53 PM||||ok heres the new word from the shop. the metal shaft that the sprocket goes onto broke off and fell into the tranny. they said that they took a magnet and reached down into the tranny(from the hole where the sending unit goes in) and fished out some small pieces of gear. i believe that they said that it was from the main gear in the tranny. does anyone think that a small piece of metal like that could do that much damage?? the thing is im not going to replace the tranny on that car. its not worth it. too much $$ im thinking of just buying another fiero instead. thing is the only one that i can find is an auto trans. i hate auto trans but i have to do something soon. got to get to work and school you know. by the way jeff it is the autoshop in carbondale. im posting a few pix of the sending unit that is broken. i did see what looks like small metal in the oil when i was removing the unit. i changed the fluid not too long ago so im thinking that it all started when the piece broke. help!!<P><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/broken_sending_unit.JPG"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/broken2.JPG"> <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||07-15-2000||02:13 PM||||In your case I'd be worried about the drive teeth on the worm gear that runs the sender gear. That sender shaft likely knocked chunks out of the drive worm. You need to rotate the axle and visually watch the worm gear in the tranny. You'll need to drain oil to do that. The metal they picked out could be chunks of the worm gear or the sender shaft. If the worm is dead then the tranny needs to be taken apart to fix it. <P>IF the worm gear is OK, IF, then before you do anyhting else replace the sender & it's gear and see how it runs. You can do that yourself with common tools. If the tranny really is broken you'll know 5 minutes after the sender is replaced.<P>The motor WILL NOT run right w/o that gear. The ECM uses the speedo feed and will go nuts if the feed is gone. You need to reset the ECM once the gear is replaced.<P>I only have drawings of 1 5 speed. I believe the final drive is similar in all them. IF the chunk of shaft got caught between the final drive ring or the diferantial and the case it could damage it. (Most likely it'd punch it thru the tranny case.)<P>IF the tranny has survived undamaged, which I kind of doubt, then at the very least it needs to have the remains of that shaft recoverd. I'd be very suprised if at least the speedo worm gear isn't damaged. If you leave the peice of shaft in there it's big enough that it could shift/bounce into the final drive and trash it. (There's not really any way for it to get to main gears. It would want to fall into the final well.)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||BBBAD GT||07-16-2000||01:06 PM||||Erik:  There is a Fiero sitting in someones yard here in town with a for sale sign for 200 or 300.  It might have a good trans, and I bet you could take it off his hands for like $50 or so.  Email me if interested, I could go take a closer look at it.<P><P>------------------<BR>87 GT, Stock for now<BR><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT's Web Site</A>||||reg||1
